The method toArray returns an array copy of elementData up to size.

The comment: "For every valid index i (from 0 to the size of the list), if the element at index i in the list is null, then the element at index i in the returned array is also null; otherwise, the element in the list at index i is equal to the element at index i in the returned array."

So we need a universal quantifier over i: 0 <= i && i < this.size() (size after operation). Since method returns array, we need to refer to the result array: \result.

We need condition: (this.get(i) == null) => \result[i] == null, and (! (this.get(i) == null)) => this.get(i).equals(\result[i])? Actually equality: element in list equals element in array. Since they are objects, we can use \result[i].equals(this.get(i))? But we need to be careful about null. So we can express: (this.get(i) == null) ? \result[i] == null : this.get(i).equals(\result[i]).

In JML style we might write: \forall int i; 0 <= i && i < this.size(); (this.get(i) == null ? \result[i] == null : this.get(i).equals(\result[i]));

But using implication: (this.get(i) == null) => \result[i] == null && (this.get(i) != null) => this.get(i).equals(\result[i]).

Combine with &&.

Thus final assert:

assert \forall int i; 0 <= i && i < this.size(); (this.get(i) == null => \result[i] == null) && (this.get(i) != null => this.get(i).equals(\result[i]));

Check precedence: need parentheses around whole quantifier expression? Usually assert \forall int i; cond; body;.

So:

assert \forall int i; 0 <= i && i < this.size(); (this.get(i) == null => \result[i] == null) && (this.get(i) != null => this.get(i).equals(\result[i]));

Make sure to use \result[i] syntactically allowed? In JML you can use \result[i] for array result. Yes.

